<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wareham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wareham is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the rivers Frome and Piddle. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> settlement with a minster church, it was occupied by the Danish army in 876 and later fortified as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>'s burhs, and its earthen ramparts are among the best preserved in England. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beorhtric-of-wessex">Beorhtric of Wessex</a> was buried there in 802.</p></div>
